WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.358
ANIMA is a harness that lets an AI agent

2
00:00:03.358 --> 00:00:05.900
attempt a real bug fix,

3
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.426
and then checks whether the fix was right.

4
00:00:08.976 --> 00:00:10.570
Agents say, done.

5
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.982
But were they right?

6
00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:14.954
Somebody still has to check, by hand.

7
00:00:15.404 --> 00:00:18.091
Here is a small demo bug report.

8
00:00:18.091 --> 00:00:21.855
The function says Sunday is not part of the weekend.

9
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:26.498
A stand-in agent changes the code, and says: done.

10
00:00:26.848 --> 00:00:28.953
Now the real checker runs.

11
00:00:28.953 --> 00:00:33.910
It holds tests that the agent did not see, and cannot edit.

12
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.753
Result: fail.

13
00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:38.679
The change counted Friday as weekend.

14
00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:41.506
The agent said done, and it was wrong.

15
00:00:42.256 --> 00:00:44.901
The agent can also try to edit the tests,

16
00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:47.088
to make itself pass.

17
00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:50.206
The checker spots that, and rejects it.

18
00:00:50.956 --> 00:00:53.373
So the next rung tries again.

19
00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:56.058
A rung is just the next agent up:

20
00:00:56.058 --> 00:01:00.178
a small local model, then Claude, then Codex.

21
00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:04.002
This time, the checker says: pass.

22
00:01:04.852 --> 00:01:08.321
We ran this on nine real bug-fix jobs.

23
00:01:08.321 --> 00:01:10.522
Seven were right the first time.

24
00:01:10.522 --> 00:01:12.738
Two said done, and were wrong.

25
00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:16.882
The checker caught both, and the next rung fixed them.

26
00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:20.843
Here is one of those two, from the recorded log.

27
00:01:20.843 --> 00:01:24.281
The first agent said done. The checker disagreed.

28
00:01:24.281 --> 00:01:26.218
The next rung passed.

29
00:01:27.068 --> 00:01:28.832
To check the record yourself,

30
00:01:28.832 --> 00:01:30.948
you need Python and git.

31
00:01:30.948 --> 00:01:33.578
No account, and no keys.

32
00:01:34.028 --> 00:01:37.034
First, copy the repository.

33
00:01:38.484 --> 00:01:40.092
Then run the check.

34
00:01:40.092 --> 00:01:43.472
Each record in the log points back to the one before it,

35
00:01:43.472 --> 00:01:46.098
and here every link matches.

36
00:01:46.448 --> 00:01:49.405
Now change a single character in a copy.

37
00:01:49.405 --> 00:01:50.894
The break shows.

38
00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.852
This checks the record.

39
00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:55.574
It does not re-run the nine jobs.

40
00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.594
On our laptop, a self-test must fail

41
00:01:59.594 --> 00:02:01.156
when we plant a fault.

42
00:02:01.156 --> 00:02:03.626
Publishing it is the next step.

43
00:02:04.476 --> 00:02:07.987
What works today is the harness and its checker,

44
00:02:07.987 --> 00:02:10.473
tested for a single kind of job:

45
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:13.074
fixing a bug against hidden tests.

46
00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:15.926
It is not a general job scheduler.

47
00:02:16.576 --> 00:02:18.652
Other parts are prototypes.

48
00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:22.630
SST tries many candidate fixes.

49
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.883
STC turns a written spec into code.

50
00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:30.118
And a shared memory with TRACE, linked by a small test.

51
00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:32.590
We do not show them here.

52
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.619
To try it, start at the ANIMA repository on GitHub.

53
00:02:37.619 --> 00:02:39.830
The address is on screen.

54
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.388
A checker the agent cannot see or edit decides.

55
00:02:44.388 --> 00:02:49.022
And if the agent was wrong, the next rung tries again.

